      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Choosing between an SGLT2 inhibitor and a GLP-1 receptor agonist as a first-line or early treatment option for type 2 diabetes depends on individual patient risk factors and organ-specific goals rather than a one-size-fits-all hierarchy . While metformin has traditionally been the default first-line therapy, current guidelines from organizations like the American Diabetes Association (ADA) allow SGLT2 inhibitors or GLP-1 receptor agonists to be used early or as part of a tailored approach based on comorbid cardiorenal disease and weight management needs.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "depends on individual patient risk factors and organ-specific goals rather than a one-size-fits-all hierarchy"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1,
          2,
          3
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Core Comparison"
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "How to Choose (Clinical Priority)"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Choose an SGLT2 Inhibitor First if:",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "The patient has a history of heart failure (especially reduced ejection fraction)."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "The patient has chronic kidney disease (CKD) with protein/albuminuria or declining eGFR."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "An oral, once-daily pill is strongly preferred over an injection.",
                "reference_indexes": [
                  3,
                  4,
                  6,
                  7
                ]
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              3,
              4,
              6,
              7
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Choose a GLP-1 Receptor Agonist First if:",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "The patient needs robust weight loss or management of obesity-related comorbidities."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "The patient has a high risk of stroke or established atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease (ASCVD) where major adverse cardiovascular event reduction is the top priority."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "A greater lowering of HbA1c is required.",
                "reference_indexes": [
                  1,
                  3,
                  6,
                  7,
                  8
                ]
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              3,
              6,
              7,
              8
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Combination Therapy:",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "In many high-risk patients, current clinical evidence supports combining both classes, as they provide complementary mechanisms\u2014improving glucose control, weight, and blood pressure with a low risk of hypoglycemia.",
                "reference_indexes": [
                  5,
                  7,
                  9,
                  10
                ]
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              5,
              7,
              9,
              10
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
